    "body": "\n## Summary\n\nOn MI355X (gfx950, CDNA4), AITER's Triton MXFP4 GEMM (`aiter::gemm_afp4wfp4_`) streams three things from HBM: the packed FP4 operands, one E8M0 scale byte per 32 K-elements for each operand, and the BF16 output. The kernel feeds the scales to the scaled MFMA instructions (`V_MFMA_SCALE_F32_16X16X128_F8F6F4` / `32X32X64`), so they are real HBM traffic. The TraceLens perf model counted only the operands and the output, and its docstring called the scales negligible. This was confirmed via AITER runs. Note `hipblaslt_gemm_fp4` and `aiter_gemm_a4w4` already count scale bytes. At small decode sizes these GEMMs are HBM-bound on MI355X. \n\n`bytes()` now adds `x_scales` and `w_scales`, using the shapes and dtypes the trace records at `Input Dims[2:4]` and `Input type[2:4]`. FLOPs stay at 2·M·N·K. The scaled MFMA applies the scales inside the instruction, and the MXFP4 peak is quoted on 2·M·N·K.\n\n## Examples\n\nBytes per call, before and after the fix (BF16 output):\n\n| M | N | K | Before (MB) | After (MB) | Scale bytes (MB) | Increase |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 7168 | 7168 | 25.71 | 27.31 | 1.61 | +6.2% |\n| 16 | 7168 | 7168 | 25.98 | 27.59 | 1.61 | +6.2% |\n| 64 | 2112 | 7168 | 8.07 | 8.56 | 0.49 | +6.0% |\n| 256 | 7168 | 2048 | 11.27 | 11.75 | 0.48 | +4.2% |\n| 4096 | 7168 | 7168 | 99.09 | 101.61 | 2.52 | +2.5% |\n\nThe scales add (M + N)·K/32 bytes against (M + N)·K/2 for the packed operands, so the increase approaches 6.25% when operand reads dominate. It shrinks at large M, where the output write takes a bigger share. T\n\n## What changed\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `TraceLens/PerfModel/extensions/perf_model_extensions.py` | `gemm_afp4wfp4`: read the two scale shapes and dtypes in `get_param_details`, add their bytes in `bytes()`, and update the FLOPs and Bytes sections of the docstring |\n| `tests/test_perfmodel_extensions.py` | `test_gemm_afp4wfp4`: use a consistent M=64, N=256, K=256 event with `unsigned char` scales, as the profiler records them, and assert the exact byte count instead of `> 0` |\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [x] `pytest tests/test_perfmodel_extensions.py`: 306 passed\n- [x] `black --check` clean on both files\n- [x] Profiled a real `aiter::gemm_afp4wfp4_` call (M=16, N=K=7168) with `torch.profiler` and passed the event to the model. The recorded scale entries are `[16, 224]` and `[7168, 224]`, typed `unsigned char`. The model returns 27,586,048 bytes, matching the hand count; it returned 25,976,832 before the fix.\n\n## Net diff\n\n```\n2 files changed, 25 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)\n```\n",
